  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe current approach uses simulated annealing with a fixed pattern and local perturbations. While simple, this method tends to get stuck in local optima. We'll implement a population-based optimization using the Covariance Matrix Adaptation Evolution Strategy (CMA-ES), which is better suited for high-dimensional optimization problems with expensive function evaluations. CMA-ES maintains a population distribution that adapts based on past successes, allowing for stronger exploration and exploitation. We'll also introduce a repair mechanism that maintains integral constraints and uses grid-weighted sorting similar to the initial method to improve symmetry.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n- Representation: Vector of length n_points representing function samples over [0,2]\n- Objective: Minimize C5 = max_k \u2232h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n- Constraints:\n  - 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n  - \u2211h[i] * dx = 0.5 (where dx=2/n_points)\n- Repair function:\n  - Sort grid indices by distance from center (1/|i-center| + 1e-6)\n  - Sort candidate values in descending order\n  - Scale by factor: target_sum / current_sum\n  - Clip values to [0,1]\n- Objective computation:\n  - Double input array for boundary handling\n  - Compute full correlation between h and 1-h\n  - Multiply maximum by dx to get area-based C5\n\n3. Implementation procedure\na. Initialize parameters:\n   - n_points = 280 (initial fixed)\n   - dx = 2.0/n_points\n   - Compute center position = n_points//2\n   - Precompute weights = 1/(abs(indices-center)+1e-6) for repair\n\nb. Repair function:\n   - Input vector h\n   - Map to grid positions\n   - Reassign sorted values by descending grid weight\n   - Scale to target integral (n_points/4)\n   - Clip values to [0,1]\n   - Handle integral deficit by setting low-grid values to 1\n\nc. Objective function:\n   - Apply repair to candidate\n   - Compute correlation as:\n        h_double = concatenate([h, reversed(h)])\n        corr = correlate(h_double, 1-h_double, mode='full')\n        return max(corr) * dx\n\nd. Optimization using CMA-ES:\n   - Start with initial solution from parent or random [0.3,0.7] values\n   - Set covariance matrix initial standard deviation\n   - Max evaluations: budget_s*10 (conservative estimate)\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n- Initial mean: \n  - If parent available, use grid-weighted parent\n  - Otherwise uniform [0.3,0.7] values\n- Standard deviation: \n  - Initial \u03c3 = 0.1 * (n_points**0.5)\n  - Dynamic adjustment allowed\n- Repair variations: \n  - Different weight function\n  - Progressive clipping strategy\n- Population size: sqrt(n_points) (approximately 16 samples)\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n- Baseline estimation: ~1000 function evaluations\n- Time estimation: 300ms per evaluation \u2192 5 mins total\n- Function evaluation timing:\n  - Repair: O(n log n) (dominated component)\n  - Correlation: C(n_points log n_points) via fast convolution\n- Stopping conditions:\n  - Budget exceeded\n  - No improvement after 10 evaluations\n- Graceful degradation:\n  - Fallback to initial solution\n  - Dynamic repair skips clipping if values already within bounds\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- Repair produces feasible solutions:\n  - All values within [0,1]\n  - Integral within 1% of target\n- Final validation:\n  - Re-run objective to confirm feasible solution\n  - Compute C5 for final candidate\n- Output contract:\n  - h_values = repaired final solution\n  - c5_bound = minimum C5 achieved\n  - n_points = current representation size\n- Validation checks:\n  - Integral_match = abs(actual - 0.5)/0.5 < 0.01\n  - All_values = np.max(h) \u2264 1 and np.min(h) \u2265 0\n  - Correlation finite and real\n\nImplementation:\n- Use numpy and CMA-ES library\n- Parameters: n_points=280 fixed initially\n- Repair maps [0,1]\u00d7n_points to feasible solutions preserving integral\n- Objective transforms solution to C5 value\n- CMA-ES minimizes the C5 function\n- Performance metric improvement goal: below 0.380876\n\nThe CMA-ES approach is chosen because it is:\n- More effective for complex search spaces\n- Less likely to get stuck than simulated annealing\n- Proven for expensive black-box optimization",
